hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the key  distinctions that st. Augustine of Hippo  makes in book one of on free choice of  the will is that between two  fundamentally different kinds of law one  of which we very easily recognize  because we're governed by it and we  think of ourselves as operating within  its confines and that is what he would  call temporal law if we recognize some  Universal standards of morality that we  think don't differ from place to place  and time to time then we also recognize  what he calls the eternal law and in  Augustine's work the eternal law has a  priority over the temporal law so this  is a very important distinction he tells  us that the eternal law is the what he  calls the law of reason and it cannot  change the temporal law by by contrast  can in fact change and will vary from  place to place and situation to  situation as the matter of fact it  always didn't even brings up cases where  the the temporal law must change to  adapt to new circumstances and he  is that here we go this is in section 15  will not any man intelligent man regard  that law as unchangeable and eternal  which is termed the law of reason we  must always obey it he says it is the  law through which wicked people deserve  an unhappy and good people a happy life  and through the which the law we have  said should be called temporal is  rightly decreed and rightly changed so  there is a dependency of the temporal  law upon the eternal law the eternal law  provides us with the criteria by which  the temporal law should be made and the  temporal law should be modified so he  tells us a little bit later that what is  just and what is lawful in the temporal  law actually derives from the eternal  law and he doesn't clarify it much more  at that point but he also says something  else that's very interesting a little  bit earlier in the work he says that a  law which is not just does not appear to  actually be a law at all there is  something of the very nature of law that  requires that it be just so there could  be laws that are as we say on the books  or you know laws in the sense of orders  from authorities that are not actually  law they're not actually binding because  they're not just when they are in  conflict with the eternal law when they  command us to do things that we know are  wrong or when they forbid us to do  things that we know we ought to do then  we've got a serious problem there and so  Augustine is articulating that what does  he mean by the the temporal law now he's  not giving you a whole treatise on law  here but he does have a couple very  interesting examples of what its scope  would be and how it might be changed so  it's well worth looking and at those  let's take a look at three particular  discussions that he has so in section 12  of this this work he tells us that  the temporal law may in fact allow its  subjects to to engage in killing to  prevent some other types of harms so for  example this is actually in 11 and then  in 212 he says the law you know is the  law just which authorizes a traveler to  kill a robber and self-protection or any  man or woman to kill an assailant if  possible before the violence has been  carried out what about laws that order a  soldier to kill the enemy if they refuse  they're punished by military authorities  can we call these laws unjust or no laws  at all and his a student of odious says  I see pretty well that a law which gives  its subjects permission to commit lesser  crimes in order to prevent greater ones  has a good defense against an accusation  of this kind it is a much lesser evil  for the assassin than for the man who  defends his own life to be killed so  we're saying that because somebody is  engaging in aggression or planning  aggression it's unfortunate that  somebody is killed but better that that  person is killed the guilty rather than  the innocent or in the case of the  soldier he says here we go when a  soldier kills the enemy he is enforcing  the law and so has no difficulty in  carrying out his duty without passion  the law itself which is issued to  protect a subject cannot be convicted of  passion if its author issued it in  obedience to God's will that is to  fulfill eternal justice he may have done  so without any passion of all even if he  issued it out of passion it does not  follow that the law need be carried out  with passion because a good law can be  issued by a man who is not good so you  know this this is an interesting  discussion here this always comes up by  the way when people talk about you know  the Ten Commandments and thou shalt not  kill was in one way in which that gets  interpreted thou shalt not commit murder  and so some forms of killing for example  self-defense might although there's  still not good in some respect they're  not as bad  as you know like going around and just  killing somebody and so the temporal law  could make these sorts of distinctions  and that could be in accordance with  eternal law he also talks a little bit  later on another great example this is  in section 14 about governments because  governments are structured by law as  well  he said let's look at the law which is  put forth in writing is is it for the  good of people living in this present  life the answer is yes it's to help us  out it's allow us to live together so he  says do these peoples and States belong  to the class of things which cannot  perish or change  are they altogether everlasting or are  they subject to time and change and his  student says well clearly they're  subject to time and change so here's  where we get to the crux if a people is  well disciplined and observant of social  good and such that every individual puts  public before private interest is not  this people rightly granted by law  authority to elect its own officials to  govern its Fairs that has affairs of the  state we're talking about representative  government representative government in  order to be for the common good actually  requires that the people who are doing  the voting think about the common good  not just their own interests not just  their own prejudices not just their own  desires but that of the entire society  and sometimes be willing to sacrifice  their own interest for the betterment of  the totality if that's not the case then  according to Augustine maybe that people  doesn't actually deserve to rule itself  so he goes on and he says if the people  deteriorates and prefers private to  public interest and sells its vote for  bribes and is corrupted by ambitious  politicians and puts into power  criminals with no sense of honor would  not any honest man a sufficient  influences left be justified in  depriving this people of self-government  and putting them under the authority of  a few honest people or even of one and  Amodeo says yes they would be so you  know that would be another configuration  of  parole law if the people can't govern  itself and it's gonna screw it up and  inevitably maybe it's better to have a  monarchy maybe it's better to have a you  know triumvirate or something like that  right  didn't we actually work out that well  for the Romans and Augustine knows that  now another thing that he discussed is  much later on and this is towards the  end of the work he's talking about the  good of the you know that we call  temporal so wealth and bodily things and  freedom and you know honors in the state  and all these sorts of things he says  how the law signs to each person their  share would be a long and difficult  matter to explain one plainly not  necessary for our purpose we need only  notice that the power of this law to  enforce itself does not extend further  than to take away and confiscate as a  punishment those things or a part of  them it brings pressure to bear through  fear and to gain an end turns and twist  the souls of the unhappy people for  whose government it is fitted they can  be you know kept in in line so you know  the the government allows sort of a  distribution of goods and it also can  take away those things and that's what  the temporal law one of its functions is  in fact what about the eternal law what  is Augustine tell us about it besides  the fact that there is an eternal law  and that the temporal law should be  looking to the eternal law in and being  structured and being changed so one  thing it right off the bat is he tells  us and this is in the same section that  the eternal law tells us human beings to  prefer eternal goods to temporal ones so  we should be less concerned about making  money or our financial status or bodily  health or you know our social status or  all of our you know relations or where  we live or go on and on and on and on  right what school we get to go to not  that those aren't good things or you  know or important things we should be  less concerned about those than we are  about eternal Goods so what are eternal  Goods possession of the virtues justice  fortitude temperance wisdom right or  prudence we ought to value that we ought  to value having a good will we ought to  value the eternal law itself above all  of these other things which means that  it doesn't say that we can't have these  other things but when push comes to  shove we should give eternal law the  preference eternal law is saying eternal  law has to be first another thing he  tells us is that everything should have  its due order and the way that this is  being translated is actually expressing  a kind of adverb or do not assume I  ordered to the greatest degree or most  ordered or most well ordered right and  that should extend to ourselves we  should actually have our rational part  running the show and not just our base  desires or you know the lusts of the  body or whatever it it's going to be and  we should also be extending that into  our fabrics of relationships within a  society including the temporal law  finally he tells us another thing about  the structure of the eternal law how it  in fact you could say  structures the the universe itself ah  here we go  hey this is in section 30 he says the  eternal law has settled this with  unchangeable firmness it is settled that  merit lies in the will whether we  deserve good things are not lies in our  Faculty of will and reward and  punishment lie in happiness and misery  so we can understand the eternal law not  just as like a set of norms where you're  like you know you should do this and you  know don't do this and then it sort of  comes down and imposes itself on our  norms it's the very fabric of the the  universe itself including how it is  in our human nature the will works and  we can either reject the eternal wall  and say I'm gonna you know try to see if  I can do things differently I'm sure  that I can have all the temporal goods  and also satisfy the eternal law because  I'm an exception to the rule we can do  that if we want to but we can't avoid  suffering the consequences of doing that  so this is a very important distinction  in Augustine's work and hopefully now  it's a little bit more clear what the  difference between these two sorts of  laws actually resides  you
 

